n FIFO\n  maxConcurrency: number;\n  activeCount: number;\n  middleware: Middleware[];\n  handler?: Handler;\n}\n```\n\nWait, let me reconsider. The task should be in a global registry for lookup by ID, and the queue should reference tasks. But tasks also need to be in the queue for ordering.\n\nActually, let me keep it simpler. Each NamedQueue has its own list of pending task IDs. The Task objects live in the global registry.\n\nLet me think about the structure more carefully...\n\nI think the cleanest approach is:\n1. A `TaskStore` that holds all tasks by ID\n2. `NamedQueue` objects that manage the ordering of task IDs\n3. The main `TaskQueueSystem` class that ties everything together\n\nFor the queue ordering, I need to efficiently get the \"next\" task considering:\n- Priority (1-10, lower is higher)\n- Available time (delayed tasks)\n- Dependencies (blocked tasks)\n- FIFO (createdAt for ties)\n\nThe simplest approach: when getting the next task, iterate through the pending tasks sorted by (priority, createdAt, availableAt) and return the first one that is available (not delayed, not blocked, and concurrency allows).\n\nWait, the sorting should be: available tasks first, then by priority, then by FIFO. But delayed tasks that are now available should be mixed in based on their ordering.\n\nLet me re-read phase 4: \"When multiple delayed tasks become available simultaneously, they should be ordered by availability time, then priority, then FIFO.\"\n\nSo the ordering is:\n1. Available now vs not available (available tasks first)\n2. Among available tasks: priority (lower = higher), then FIFO\n3. Among delayed tasks becoming available: availability time, then priority, then FIFO\n\nActually, I think the simplest correct approach is:\n- When retrieving the next task, find all tasks that are available (availableAt <= now, dependencies met, concurrency allows)\n- Among those, sort by (priority, createdAt) and return the first one\n- If no tasks are available, return null\n\nThis handles all cases correctly. The key insight is that \"non-delayed tasks are always preferred over delayed tasks that are not yet available\" - so we just filter by availability.\n\nLet me now plan the file structure:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  index.ts          - Main exports\n  types.ts          - Type definitions\n  task.ts           - Task class/interface\n  queue.ts          - NamedQueue class\n  system.ts         - TaskQueueSystem class\n  uuid.ts           - UUID generation\n  cron.ts           - Cron expression parsing\n```\n\nActually, to keep it simpler and since we don't have that much code, maybe I'll just use fewer files. Let me think...\n\nI'll go with:\n```\nsrc/\n  index.ts          - Main exports and TaskQueueSystem class\n  types.ts          - All type definitions\n  queue.ts          - NamedQueue implementation\n  cron.ts           - Cron parsing\n  uuid.ts           - UUID generation\n```\n\nWait, I need to be careful about circular dependencies. Let me think about what depends on what.\n\nThe TaskQueueSystem needs NamedQueue. NamedQueue needs to know about Task types. The cron module is standalone.\n\nLet me just do a clean separation:\n\n```\nsrc/\n  index.ts          - Main exports\n  types.ts          - All type definitions and interfaces\n  queue.ts          - NamedQueue class\n  system.ts         - TaskQueueSystem class (main API)\n  cron.ts           - Cron expression parsing\n  uuid.ts           - UUID generation\n```\n\nOK let me start implementing. I'll write tests alongside the implementation.\n\nFor UUID generation, I can use `crypto.randomUUID()` which is available in Node.js 19+. Since we're targeting ES2022 and Node 16+, I should check. Actually, `crypto.randomUUID()` is available from Node 14.17+. So that should work.\n\nActually wait, let me check if we have the crypto module available. Since we're using `@types/node`, we should be fine. Let me use `crypto.randomUUID()`.\n\nLet me now think about the test structure. I'll create a