askId: string): Task | undefined;\n  size(queue?: string): number;\n  listQueues(): string[];\n  setConcurrency(queue: string, max: number): void;\n  getConcurrency(queue: string): { max: number; active: number };\n  getDeadLetterQueue(queue: string): Task[];\n  retryFromDeadLetter(taskId: string): Task;\n  clearDeadLetterQueue(queue: string): void;\n  reportProgress(taskId: string, percent: number, message?: string): void;\n  onProgress(taskId: string, callback: (percent: number, message?: string) => void): () => void;\n  addSchedule(id: string, cron: string, queue: string, payload: unknown, options?: AddTaskOptions): void;\n  removeSchedule(id: string): void;\n  listSchedules(): Schedule[];\n  tick(now?: number): void;\n  use(queue: string, middleware: Middleware): void;\n  process(queue: string, handler: Handler): void;\n  shutdown(timeout?: number): Promise<ShutdownResult>;\n  onShutdown(callback: () => void | Promise<void>): void;\n  getDependencyStatus(taskId: string): DependencyStatus;\n}\n```\n\nLet me now write the implementation. I'll be very careful about the interactions between features.\n\nKey interactions to consider:\n1. Priority + Delay: When a delayed task becomes available, it should be ordered by priority among other available tasks\n2. Priority + Dependencies: Blocked tasks are skipped, unblocked tasks sorted by priority\n3. Retry + DLQ: Failed tasks with retries go back to queue, exhausted tasks go to DLQ\n4. Retry + Delay: Retried tasks have backoff delay\n5. Concurrency + Priority: Get highest-priority task subject to concurrency\n6. Concurrency + Complete/Fail: Freeing concurrency slot\n7. Cancellation + Retry: Cancelled tasks are never retried\n8. Dependencies + Complete/Fail: Completing a task checks dependents, failing doesn't auto-fail dependents\n9. Middleware + Handler: Middleware wraps the handler\n10. Shutdown + Everything: Stop accepting, wait for processing, force-cancel\n\nLet me start implementing now. I'll write all the code, then tests, then verify.\n\nActually, per the instructions, I should write tests first. Let me write tests as I go.\n\nLet me think about how to structure the implementation files. I want to keep it manageable.\n\nI think I'll go with:\n- `src/types.ts` - All types, interfaces, enums\n- `src/uuid.ts` - UUID generation\n- `src/cron.ts` - Cron expression parsing\n- `src/queue.ts` - NamedQueue class (internal)\n- `src/system.ts` - TaskQueueSystem class (main public API)\n- `src/index.ts` - Re-exports\n\nAnd tests:\n- `tests/task-queue.test.ts` - All tests\n\nLet me start writing the implementation now. I'll be methodical about it.\n\nFirst, let me think about the complete types:\n\n```typescript\nexport enum TaskStatus {\n  Pending = 'pending',\n  Processing = 'processing',\n  Completed = 'completed',\n  Failed = 'failed',\n  Cancelled = 'cancelled',\n}\n\nexport interface TaskProgress {\n  percent: number;\n  message?: string;\n}\n\nexport interface Task {\n  id: string;\n  payload: unknown;\n  status: TaskStatus;\n  priority: number;\n  createdAt: number;\n  availableAt: number;\n  queueName: string;\n  attempts: number;\n  maxRetries: number;\n  baseDelay: number;\n  dependsOn: string[];\n  progress: TaskProgress;\n  error?: unknown;\n  failureCount: number;\n}\n\nexport interface AddTaskOptions {\n  queue?: string;\n  priority?: number;\n  delay?: number;\n  availableAt?: Date | number;\n  maxRetries?: number;\n  baseDelay?: number;\n  dependsOn?: string[];\n}\n\nexport type Middleware = (task: Task, next: () => void) => void;\nexport type Handler = (task: Task) => void | Promise<void>;\n\nexport interface Schedule {\n  id: string;\n  cron: string;\n  queueName: string;\n  payload: unknown;\n  options: Omit<AddTaskOptions, 'queue'>;\n  lastRun?: number;\n}\n\nexport interface DependencyStatus {\n  taskId: string;\n  dependsOn: string[];\n  resolved: string[];\n  pending: string[];\n}\n\nexport interface ShutdownResult {\n  completed: number;\n  forceCancelled: number;\n  durationMs: number;\n}\n```\n\nNow let me think about the NamedQueue. It needs to:\n- Store pending task IDs\n- Track active (processing) count\n- Have a max concurrency\n- Have middleware and handler\n\nFor getting the next task, I need to:\n1. Sort pending tasks by priority, then createdAt\n2. Filter by availability (availableAt <= now)\n3. Filter by dependencies (all dependsOn completed)\n4. Check concurrency\n5. Return the first matching task\n\nSince we need to do this filtering on every processNext call, I could either:\na) Maintain a sorted data structure and filter on retrieval\nb) Sort on every retrieval\n\nGiven the in-memory nature and the complexity of the filtering, I'll sort on every retrieval. For small to medium queue sizes, this is fine.\n\nActually, let me think more carefully. The queue stores task IDs. When we call processNext, we need to:\n1. Get all pending tasks from the queue\n2. Filter to available ones (not delayed, not blocked)\n3. Sort by priority, then createdAt\n4. Check concurrency\n5. Return the first one\n\nThis is O(n log n) per retrieval, which is acceptable for an in-memory system.\n\nLet me now think about the complete NamedQueue:\n\n```typescript\nclass NamedQueue {\n  name: string;\n  pendingIds: string[] = [];\n  activeCount: number = 0;\n  maxConcurrency: number = Infinity;\n  middleware: Middleware[] = [];\n  handler?: Handler;\n  \n  // DLQ\n  deadLetterTasks: Task[] = [];\n}\n```\n\nWait, should the DLQ be part of NamedQueue or the system? Since each named queue has its own DLQ, it makes sense to put it in NamedQueue. But tasks in the DLQ need to be accessible globally too for retryFromDeadLetter.\n\nLet me keep DLQ in NamedQueue but have the system provide the public API.\n\nNow, for the global task store: I need to be able to look up tasks by ID. The TaskQueueSystem will have a `Map<string, Task>` for this. When a task moves to DLQ, it stays in the global store.\n\nLet me think about the retryFromDeadLetter flow:\n1. Find the task in the DLQ\n2. Remove it from DLQ\n3. Create a new task (or reset the existing one?)\n4. Add it back to the queue\n\nThe spec says \"move a dead-lettered task back to its original queue for a fresh round of retries.\" This suggests creating a new task or resetting the existing one. I think creating a new task makes more sense since it gets a new ID and fresh state. But actually, \"move\" suggests keeping the same task. Let me re-read...\n\n\"It should be possible to move a dead-lettered task back to its original queue for a fresh round of retries.\"\n\nI think this means the task goes back with fresh retry state. I'll reset the attempts and failure count, change status back to pending, and re-add to the queue. Or I could create a new task. Let me think...\n\nActually, for simplicity, I'll create a new task with the same payload and queue but fresh retry state. The returned task will have a new ID.\n\nWait, but the spec says \"move a dead-lettered task back\" - this implies the same task. Let me keep the same task but reset its state. Actually, I think it's cleaner to return a new task. The old one was \"dead\" and this is a \"fresh round.\" Let me just create a new task.\n\nHmm, but then what about the old task in the DLQ? We'd remove it and add a new one. That seems clean.\n\nOK, let me also think about how `processNext` interacts with middleware and handlers.\n\nPhase 11 says:\n- Middleware runs before the handler\n- Each middleware receives task and next()\n- If middleware doesn't call next, processing is skipped\n- If middleware throws, task is marked as failed\n\nSo the flow is:\n1. Get the next available task (status -> processing)\n2. Run through middleware chain\n3. If all middleware call next, run the handler\n4. Handler completes -> complete or fail the task\n\nBut wait, the middleware and handler are async potentially. How does `processNext` work then? Does it return the task immediately (status processing) and then run the middleware/handler in the background?\n\nI think `processNext` should return the task in processing state, and the middleware/handler execution should happen separately. The `processNext` just returns the next task and changes its status to processing. The middleware/handler is a separate concern.\n\nWait, re-reading phase 11: \"When a task is processed, it passes through all registered middleware in order before reaching the final handler.\"\n\nSo the middleware wraps the processing. When you call `processNext`, the task goes through middleware and then the handler. But if the handler is async, when does `processNext` return?\n\nI think the design should be:\n- `processNext` returns the task (in processing state) for manual handling\n- If a handler is registered, `processNext` also triggers the handler (and middleware) asynchronously\n- The task is auto-completed or auto-failed based on the handler result\n\nActually, let me reconsider. The handler and middleware system seems to be an alternative to manual processing. If you register a handler, the system processes tasks automatically through the handler. If you don't, you manually call `processNext` and then `complete`/`fail`.\n\nI think the cleanest design is:\n- `processNext` always returns the next available task and marks it as processing\n- If a handler is registered, the task also goes through middleware -> handler automatically\n- If the handler succeeds (no throw), the task is auto-completed\n- If the handler throws, the task is auto-failed\n- If no handler is registered, the caller is responsible for calling complete/fail\n\nThis way, the handler/middleware system is an optional automation layer on top of the manual system.\n\nOK, I think I have enough of a design to start implementing. Let me write the code now.\n\nOne more thing: for the cron parsing, I need to support:\n- `*/N * * * *` -- every N minutes\n- `0 * * * *` -- every hour on the hour\n- `0 0 * * *` -- every day at midnight\n\nI'll implement a simple parser