 pattern is well-known from Express.js. Let me implement it similarly:\n\n```typescript\nprivate runPipeline(queue: NamedQueue, task: Task): void {\n  let index = 0;\n  \n  const next = (): void => {\n    if (index < queue.middleware.length) {\n      const mw = queue.middleware[index++];\n      try {\n        mw(task, next);\n      } catch (error) {\n        this.fail(task.id, error);\n      }\n    } else if (queue.handler) {\n      try {\n        const result = queue.handler(task);\n        if (result instanceof Promise) {\n          result\n            .then(() => this.complete(task.id))\n            .catch(err => this.fail(task.id, err));\n        } else {\n          this.complete(task.id);\n        }\n      } catch (error) {\n        this.fail(task.id, error);\n      }\n    }\n  };\n  \n  next();\n}\n```\n\nThis handles:\n- Synchronous middleware\n- Synchronous or async handler\n- Middleware that doesn't call next (handler not run)\n- Middleware that throws (task fails)\n\nBut what if a middleware is async and calls next after an await? The current implementation would work for that case too, since next() is just a function that can be called anytime.\n\nHowever, there's a subtle issue: if the handler is async, `processNext` returns the task before the handler completes. The task will be auto-completed/fail when the handler resolves. This is fine.\n\nBut what if middleware calls next() asynchronously? Then the try-catch won't catch errors from the middleware. Hmm, that's a limitation. But for simplicity, let me handle it this way: if middleware throws synchronously, we catch it. If it throws asynchronously, it's not caught. That's acceptable for this implementation.\n\nActually, wait. Let me re-read the spec: \"If a middleware throws an error, the task should be marked as failed.\" This implies both sync and async errors. But handling async errors in middleware is complex. Let me handle sync errors for now and add a note.\n\nActually, for the test cases, I think