schedule, determines if it's due based on the current time (or the provided `now` parameter), and enqueues a task if it is.\n\nFor determining if a schedule is due:\n1. Parse the cron expression\n2. Check if the current time matches the pattern\n3. If yes and we haven't already enqueued for this time, enqueue\n\nFor tracking \"already enqueued for this time\", I'll use `lastRun` on the Schedule. If `lastRun` is set and the current minute is the same as lastRun's minute, skip.\n\nWait, but what if the cron is `0 0 * * *` (daily) and we call tick() at 00:00:00 and then at 00:00:30? Both are in the same minute, so we'd skip the second. But if we call tick() at 00:00:00 and then at 00:01:00, the minute has changed but the pattern doesn't match (it only matches at minute 0), so we'd skip the second.\n\nActually, for `0 0 * * *`, the pattern matches at 00:00 every day. If we call tick() at 00:00:30, it matches. If we call tick() again at 00:00:59, it matches but we skip because lastRun was in the same minute. If we call tick() at 00:01:00, it doesn't match (minute 1 != 0), so we skip. If we call tick() the next day at 00:00:00, it matches and we enqueue because lastRun was yesterday.\n\nThis logic seems correct. Let me implement it.\n\nFor the Schedule type:\n\n```typescript\ninterface Schedule {\n  id: string;\n  cron: string;\n  queueName: string;\n  payload: unknown;\n  options: Omit<AddTaskOptions, 'queue'>;\n  lastRun?: number;\n}\n```\n\nWhen tick(now) is called:\n1. For each schedule:\n   a. Check if now matches the cron pattern\n   b. If yes, check if we're in the same minute as lastRun\n   c. If not in the same minute, enqueue a task and update lastRun\n\nLet me implement the cron matching. I'll support:\n- `*/N * * * *` - every N minutes\n- `0 * * * *` - every hour on the hour\n- `0 0 * * *` - every day at midnight\n\nBut also more general patterns would be nice. Let me implement a general cron parser that handles the common patterns.\n\nActually, let me support any valid simple cron expression with these field types:\n- `*` (wildcard)\n- `*/N` (step)\n- `N` (exact value)\n\nThis covers the required patterns and is flexible enough.\n\n```typescript\ntype CronField = { type: 'wildcard' } | { type: 'step'; step: number } | { type: 'exact'; value: number };\n\nfunction parseCron(expr: string): [CronField, CronField, CronField, CronField, CronField] {\n  const parts = expr.trim().split(/\\s+/);\n  if (parts.length !== 5) throw new Error(`Invalid cron expression: ${expr}`);\n  return parts.map(parseCronField) as [CronField, CronField, CronField, CronField, CronField];\n}\n\nfunction parseCronField(field: string): CronField {\n  if (field === '*') return { type: 'wildcard' };\n  if (field.startsWith('*/')) return { type: 'step', step: parseInt(field.slice(2), 10) };\n  const value = parseInt(field, 10);\n  if (!isNaN(value)) return { type: 'exact', value };\n  throw new Error(`Invalid cron field: ${field}`);\n}\n\nfunction matchesCron(parsed: [CronField, CronField, CronField, CronField, CronField], date: Date): boolean {\n  const [minute, hour, dayOfMonth, month, dayOfWeek] = parsed;\n  return fieldMatches(minute, date.getMinutes()) &&\n         fieldMatches(hour, date.getHours()) &&\n         fieldMatches(dayOfMonth, date.getDate()) &&\n         fieldMatches(month, date.getMonth() + 1) &&\n         fieldMatches(dayOfWeek, date.getDay());\n}\n\nfunction fieldMatches(field: CronField, value: number): boolean {\n  switch (field.type) {\n    case 'wildcard': return true;\n    case 'step': return value % field.step === 0;\n    case 'exact': return value === field.value;\n  }\n}\n```\n\nThis should work for all the required patterns.\n\nOK, NOW I'll write the code. Let me create all the files.\n\nWait, I realize I need to handle one more edge case for the dependency system. When I add a task with dependsOn, I need to check:\n1. All dependency task IDs exist in the system\n2. No circular dependencies\n\nFor circular dependency detection, I need to do a transitive closure from the dependencies and check if the new task ID is reachable.\n\nBut the new task hasn't been added yet. So I need to check if any of the dependencies (transitively) depend on each other in a way that creates a cycle with the new task.\n\nActually, since the new task hasn't been added yet, I can check: starting from the dependencies, do any of them (transitively) depend on each other? Wait, no. The circular dependency would be: new task depends on A, and A depends on new task. But since the new task doesn't exist yet, A can't depend on it.\n\nHmm, but the spec says \"Circular dependencies (direct or indirect) must be detected and rejected at task-creation time.\" So how can there be a circular dependency at creation time if the task doesn't exist yet?\n\nThe only way a circular dependency can exist is if:\n1. Task A is created with dependsOn=[B]\n2. Task B already exists with dependsOn=[A]\n\nWait, but task A doesn't exist when we check for B's dependencies. B's dependsOn would need to reference A's ID, which doesn't exist yet.\n\nActually, I think the circular dependency check is for this scenario:\n1. Create task A (no dependencies)\n2. Create task B with dependsOn=[A]\n3. Now try to create task C with dependsOn=[B, A] - no cycle\n4. But what about: create task A with dependsOn=[B], create task B with dependsOn=[A]? This requires A and B to both not exist yet, which means A's ID wouldn't be known when creating B.\n\nHmm, but we assign IDs before adding to the queue. So:\n1. Generate ID for task A\n2. Task A has dependsOn=[B's future ID] - but we don't know B's ID yet\n\nThis doesn't work. Circular dependencies can only happen if we somehow know future IDs.\n\nWait, maybe the circular dependency check is about indirect cycles through existing tasks. For example:\n1. Create task A\n2. Create task B with dependsOn=[A]\n3. Create task C with dependsOn=[B]\n4. Now try to add dependsOn=[C] to task A - but A is already created\n\n