t, should the middleware always run (even without a handler)? The spec says \"When a task is processed, it passes through all registered middleware.\" This suggests middleware always runs. But without a handler, what happens at the end of the chain? The last middleware's next() would call... nothing?\n\nI think the most reasonable interpretation is:\n- Middleware only runs when there's a handler registered\n- The handler is the final step in the chain\n- Without a handler, the user manually handles the task\n\nLet me implement it this way.\n\nActually, re-reading: \"A processing function/handler can be registered per queue.\" This implies it's optional. And middleware intercepts the processing. So middleware without a handler doesn't make sense.\n\nLet me make middleware only apply when a handler is registered. If there's no handler, processNext just returns the task.\n\nOK, let me write the code. I need to stop overthinking and start coding.\n\nFor the test file, I want to write comprehensive tests for all 12 phases. Let me organize the tests by phase.\n\nLet me start writing the code files now.","signature":"713a93c03e0147bf841613b158f1a002"}],"model":"zai-org/GLM-5.1-FP8","stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":20727,"output_tokens":0},"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"029c07f3-c960-4e4b-bbe8-debb251dac1f","uuid":"cf65298d-feee-4f5a-b3c0-ae6154397bf6"}
