it goes to the DLQ.\nWhen a task is cancelled:\n  - If pending: remove from pending\n  - If processing: update status to 'cancelled'\n\nFor the global task map, I'll keep all tasks regardless of status.\n\nFor the queue's pending array, I'll only keep tasks with status 'pending'.\n\nLet me use arrays for pending and DLQ, and a Map for processing.\n\nActually, for simplicity, let me just store pending task IDs in an array on the NamedQueue, and look them up in the global task map. This avoids duplicating task data.\n\nLet me finalize the data structures:\n\n```typescript\nclass NamedQueue {\n  name: string;\n  pendingIds: string[];          // task IDs in pending state\n  processingIds: Set<string>;    // task IDs in processing state\n  dlqIds: string[];              // task IDs in DLQ\n  concurrency: number | null;    // null = unlimited\n  middleware: MiddlewareFn[] = [];\n  handler: TaskHandlerFn | null = null;\n  progressListeners: Map<string, Set<ProgressListener>> = new Map();\n}\n\nclass TaskQueueSystem {\n  private queues: Map<string, NamedQueue> = new Map();\n  private tasks: Map<string, Task> = new Map();\n  private schedules: Map<string, CronSchedule> = new Map();\n  private shutdownInitiated: boolean = false;\n  private shutdownCallbacks: ShutdownCallback[] = [];\n  private processingPromises: Map<string, Promise<void>> = new Map();\n  private processingResolvers: Map<string, () => void> = new Map();\n}\n```\n\nThis is clean. Let me write the code now.\n\nWait, I also need to think about the `process()` method and how it tracks processing tasks for Phase 12.\n\nWhen `process()` is called:\n1. It finds the next available task\n2. Marks it as \"processing\"\n3. If middleware/handler are registered, it kicks off the pipeline\n4. Returns the task\n\nFor Phase 12, I need to know when a processing task finishes. If there's a handler, the handler calls `complete()`/`fail()`, which resolves a promise. If there's no handler, the caller calls `complete()`/`fail()`, which also resolves a promise.\n\nSo when `process()` is called, I create a promise and store it. When `complete()`/`fail()` is called for that task, I resolve the promise. During shutdown, I wait for all processing promises.\n\nBut what if `complete()`/`fail()` is called without going through `process()`? That shouldn't happen in normal usage, but I should handle it gracefully.\n\nLet me add the promise tracking:\n\n```typescript\nprocess(queueName?: string): Task | null {\n  // ... find and return next task ...\n  \n  // Create a promise for this processing task\n  let resolver: () => void;\n  const promise = new Promise<void>((resolve) => { resolver = resolve; });\n  this.processingPromises.set(task.id, promise);\n  this.processingResolvers.set(task.id, resolver!);\n  \n  // Run middleware/handler if registered\n  if (queue.handler) {\n    this.runPipeline(task, queue);\n  }\n  \n  return task;\n}\n\ncomplete(taskId: string): void {\n  // ... mark task as completed ...\n  \n  // Resolve the processing promise\n  const resolver = this.processingResolvers.get(taskId);\n  if (resolver) {\n    resolver();\n    this.processingPromises.delete(taskId);\n    this.processingResolvers.delete(taskId);\n  }\n}\n```\n\nFor Phase 12 shutdown:\n\n```typescript\nasync shutdown(timeout: number = 30000): Promise<ShutdownSummary> {\n  this.shutdownInitiated = true;\n  const start = Date.now();\n  \n  let completed = 0;\n  let forceCancelled = 0;\n  \n  // Wait for processing tasks to complete\n  if (this.processingPromises.size > 0) {\n    const allProcessing = Promise.all(this.processingPromises.values());\n    const timeoutPromise = new Promise<void>((resolve) => \n      setTimeout(resolve, timeout)\n    );\n    \n    await Promise.race([allProcessing, timeoutPromise]);\n    \n    // Force-cancel any remaining processing tasks\n    for (const [taskId] of this.processingPromises) {\n      this.cancel(taskId);\n      forceCancelled++;\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Run shutdown callbacks\n  for (const cb of this.shutdownCallbacks) {\n    await cb();\n  }\n  \n  return {\n    completed,\n    forceCancelled,\n    durationMs: Date.now() - start,\n  };\n}\n```\n\nHmm, but I also need to count how many tasks completed normally vs were force-cancelled. Let me track this during the shutdown process.\n\nActually, I need to be more careful. When shutdown is called:\n1. Stop accepting new tasks\n2. Wait for processing tasks to finish\n3. Count those that finished normally as \"completed\"\n4. Force-cancel those that didn't finish in time\n5. Run shutdown callbacks\n6. Return summary\n\nThe \"completed\" count in the summary should be the number of tasks that completed during the shutdown wait period. And \"forceCancelled\" should be the number of tasks that were force-cancelled.\n\nLet me track the initial set of processing tasks and then check which ones completed:\n\n```typescript\nasync shutdown(timeout: number = 30000): Promise<ShutdownSummary> {\n  if (this._shutdownResult) return this._shutdownResult;\n  \n  this.shutdownInitiated = true;\n  const start = Date.now();\n  \n  // Get all currently processing task IDs\n  const processingIds = new Set<string>();\n  for (const [, queue] of this.queues) {\n    for (const id of queue.processingIds) {\n      processingIds.add(id);\n    }\n  }\n  \n  let forceCancelled = 0;\n  \n  if (processingIds.size > 0) {\n    // Wait for all processing promises or timeout\n    const promises = [...processingIds]\n      .map(id => this.processingPromises.get(id))\n      .filter((p): p is Promise<void> => p !== undefined);\n    \n    if (promises.length > 0) {\n      const allDone = Promise.all(promises);\n      const timeoutPromise = new Promise<'timeout'>(resolve => \n        setTimeout(() => resolve('timeout'), timeout)\n      );\n      \n      await Promise.race([allDone.then(() => 'done' as const), timeoutPromise]);\n    }\n    \n    // Force-cancel any remaining processing tasks\n    for (const id of processingIds) {\n      const task = this.tasks.get(id);\n      if (task && task.status === 'processing') {\n        this.cancel(id);\n        forceCancelled++;\n      }\n    }\n  }\n  \n  // Count completed during shutdown\n  const completed = processingIds.size - forceCancelled;\n  \n  // Run shutdown callbacks\n  for (const cb of this.shutdownCallbacks) {\n    await cb();\n  }\n  \n  this._shutdownResult = {\n    completed,\n    forceCancelled,\n    durationMs: Date.now() - start,\n  };\n  \n  return this._shutdownResult;\n}\n```\n\nThis looks good. Let me now write the full implementation.\n\nOne more thing: the `runPipeline` method for Phase 11. It should:\n1. Run all middleware in order\n2. Each middleware gets the task and a `next` function\n3. If a middleware doesn't call `next`, processing stops\n4. After all middleware, run the handler\n5. If a middleware throws, mark the task as failed\n\n```typescript\nprivate runPipeline(task: Task, queue: NamedQueue): void {\n  const middleware = [...queue.middleware];\n  const handler = queue.handler;\n  \n  let index = 0;\n  \n  const runNext = () => {\n    if (index < middleware.length) {\n      const mw = middleware[index++];\n      try {\n        mw(task, runNext);\n      } catch (err) {\n        this.fail(task.id, err);\n      }\n    } else if (handler) {\n      try {\n        handler(task);\n      } catch (err) {\n        this.fail(task.id, err);\n      }\n    }\n  };\n  \n  runNext();\n}\n```\n\nThis is the standard Express-style middleware chain. If a middleware doesn't call `next()`, the chain stops. If the handler is reached, it runs.\n\nOK, I'm now going to write the full code. Let me also think about the `tick()` method for Phase 10.\n\n```typescript\ntick(): void {\n  const now = Date.now();\n  for (const [, schedule] of this.schedules) {\n    const parsed = parseCron(schedule.expression);\n    if (isDue(parsed, now)) {\n      this.addTask(schedule.payload, {\n        queueName: schedule.queueName,\n        ...schedule.taskOptions,\n      });\n      schedule.lastRunAt = now;\n    }\n  }\n}\n```\n\nWait, but `isDue` checks `d.getSeconds() === 0`, which means it's only \"due\" during the first second of the minute. In a real system, `tick()` would be called by a timer. But for testing, we need a way to force evaluation.\n\nHmm, let me reconsider. The phase says \"A manual 'tick' or 'check' mechanism should evaluate all active schedules and enqueue tasks for any that are due. This is used for testing.\"\n\nSo for testing, `tick()` checks if any schedule is due NOW and enqueues tasks. The `isDue` function checks against the current time. So in tests, you'd set up a schedule, wait for the right time, and call `tick()`.\n\nBut the `isDue` function checking `getSeconds() === 0` is too restrictive. It means `tick()` only works during the first second of a minute. Let me change the `isDue` function to be more flexible. Instead of checking seconds, I'll check if the schedule should have run at or before the current time and hasn't been run yet (or was last run before this occurrence).\n\nActually, let me rethink the cron matching. The simplest approach for testing:\n\nA schedule with expression `*/5 * * * *` should trigger at minutes 0, 5, 10, 15, etc. When `tick()` is called, it checks if the current time matches the schedule's pattern and if the schedule hasn't already been triggered for this time period.\n\nFor \"hasn't been triggered\", I'll use `lastRunAt`. If the schedule was last run at a time that's in the same \"slot\" as now, don't run it again. For `*/5 * * * *`, the slots are at minutes 0, 5, 10, etc.\n\nActually, for simplicity, let me just check if the current time matches the cron pattern. For the `*/N` pattern, it matches when `minute % N === 0`. For `0 * * * *`, it matches when `minute === 0`. For `0 0 * * *`, it matches when `hour === 0 && minute === 0`.\n\nTo prevent double-triggering, I'll track `lastRunAt` and only trigger if `lastRunAt` was in a different minute (or differe